1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,837
Chapter 46: Postterm Pregnancy.

3
00:00:05,837 --> 00:00:26,587
Estimated Gestational Age Incidence Perinatal Mortality And Morbidity Pathophysiology Complications Antepartum Management Intrapartum Management References The adjectives postterm, prolonged, postdates, and postmature are used interchangeably to describe pregnancies that have exceeded a duration considered to be the upper limit of normal.

4
00:00:26,587 --> 00:00:35,825
We eschew use of the term postdates because the real issue in many postterm pregnancies is uncertainty in the estimated date of delivery (EDD).

5
00:00:35,825 --> 00:00:45,550
Postmature is reserved for the uncommon clinical fetal syndrome in which the newborn has recognizable features indicating a pathologically prolonged pregnancy.

6
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:51,500
Thus, postterm or prolonged pregnancy is our preferred term for an extended pregnancy.

7
00:00:51,500 --> 00:01:03,662
The definition of postterm pregnancy is one that exceeds 420/7 weeks or is ≥294 days from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP).

8
00:01:03,662 --> 00:01:07,425
Importantly, this is 42 “completed weeks.

9
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:18,162
Pregnancies between 411/7 and 416/7 weeks, although in the 42nd week, do not complete 42 weeks until the seventh day has elapsed.

10
00:01:18,162 --> 00:01:26,575
These are considered late term (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019e, 2020b).

11
00:01:26,575 --> 00:01:29,225
Estimated Gestational Age.

12
00:01:29,225 --> 00:01:36,062
The current definition of postterm pregnancy assumes that ovulation occurs 2 weeks after the LMP.

13
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:43,862
Thus, some pregnancies may not actually be postterm because of error in menstrual date recall or delayed ovulation.

14
00:01:43,862 --> 00:02:00,762
Even with exactly recalled menstrual dates, there still is imprecision, and first-trimester sonography is the most accurate method to establish or confirm gestational age (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b, 2020b).

15
00:02:00,762 --> 00:02:06,987
Several studies support this (Bennett, 2004; Joseph, 2007).

16
00:02:06,987 --> 00:02:19,775
If available, gestational ages calculated from the LMP and from the first accurate ultrasound are reconciled as shown in Table 14-1 (p. 248), and the EDD is recorded .

17
00:02:19,775 --> 00:02:21,462
Incidence.

18
00:02:21,462 --> 00:02:32,262
Of the 3.75 million neonates born in the United States during 2019, 0.3 percent were delivered at ≥42 weeks .

19
00:02:32,262 --> 00:02:38,437
This rate has declined because of improved pregnancy dating accuracy and earlier intervention.

20
00:02:38,437 --> 00:02:52,625
To identify predisposing factors for postterm pregnancy, one analysis of the Danish Birth Cohort found only prepregnancy body mass index ≥25 and nulliparity to be significantly associated .

21
00:02:52,625 --> 00:02:59,662
Others reported similar associations (Arrowsmith, 2011; Mission, 2015).

22
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:10,300
Nulliparas with a long midpregnancy cervical length (third or fourth quartile) are twice as likely to deliver after 42 weeks (van der Ven, 2016).

23
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:17,800
Last, the risk for adverse pregnancy outcomes in postterm pregnancies increases with advancing maternal age .

24
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:25,787
The tendency for some mothers to have repeated postterm births suggests that some prolonged pregnancies are biologically determined.

25
00:03:25,787 --> 00:03:35,337
In one study, if a mother and daughter had a prolonged pregnancy, the risk for the daughter to have a subsequent postterm pregnancy was significantly increased .

26
00:03:35,337 --> 00:03:40,125
Maternal, not paternal, genes influence prolonged pregnancy .

27
00:03:40,125 --> 00:03:47,425
For example, genes from one locus of chromosome 2q13 are associated with gestational duration .

28
00:03:47,425 --> 00:04:05,587
As discussed in Chapter 5 (p. 102), rare fetal-placental factors that predispose to postterm pregnancy include anencephaly, adrenal hypoplasia, and X-linked placental sulfatase deficiency (Ayyavoo, 2014; MacDonald, 1965).

29
00:04:05,587 --> 00:04:08,637
Perinatal Mortality And Morbidity.

30
00:04:08,637 --> 00:04:14,725
Rates of stillbirth, neonatal death, and infant morbidity all rise after the EDD.

31
00:04:14,725 --> 00:04:21,000
Data from times before widespread intervention for postterm pregnancies are most illustrative.

32
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:29,787
In two large Swedish studies, the perinatal mortality rate reached a nadir at 39 to 40 weeks’ gestation and rose thereafter .

33
00:04:29,787 --> 00:04:33,537
This trend is also reported for the United States .

34
00:04:33,537 --> 00:04:45,312
The major causes of morbidity in these studies include gestational hypertension, prolonged labor with cephalopelvic disproportion, birth injuries, and hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy .

35
00:04:45,312 --> 00:04:55,087
Similar outcomes were reported in 78,022 women with postterm pregnancies delivered before routine labor induction was adopted in Denmark .

36
00:04:55,087 --> 00:04:58,737
A slight increase in cerebral palsy rates and

37
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:10,525
two-point lower intelligence quotient (IQ) scores have been reported in postterm births (Moster, 2010; Williams, 2020; Yang, 2010).

38
00:05:10,475 --> 00:05:15,200
Conversely, data do not associate autism with postterm birth .

39
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:17,812
Figure 46-1.

40
00:05:17,812 --> 00:05:30,362
Perinatal mortality rates in late pregnancy according to gestational age of all births in Sweden during 1943–1952 compared with those during 1977–1978.

41
00:05:30,362 --> 00:05:34,737
The partially compressed scale is used for convenience in depiction.

42
00:05:34,737 --> 00:05:40,375
Adapted from Bakketeig, 1991; Lindell, 1956.

43
00:05:40,375 --> 00:05:42,937
Table 46-1.

44
00:05:42,937 --> 00:06:01,100
Adverse Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes Associated with Postterm Pregnancy Alexander and colleagues (2000a) reviewed 56,317 singleton pregnancies delivered at ≥40 weeks between 1988 and 1998 at Parkland Hospital.

45
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:06,550
Labor was induced in 35 percent of pregnancies completing 42 weeks.

46
00:06:06,550 --> 00:06:15,187
The rate of cesarean delivery for dystocia or fetal distress was significantly greater at 42 weeks compared with earlier gestations.

47
00:06:15,187 --> 00:06:20,425
More newborns of postterm pregnancies were admitted to intensive care units.

48
00:06:20,425 --> 00:06:26,275
Notably, the incidence of neonatal seizures and deaths was doubled at 42 weeks.

49
00:06:26,275 --> 00:06:39,337
Smith (2001) has challenged analyses such as these because the population at risk for perinatal mortality in a given week consists of all ongoing pregnancies rather than just the births in a given week.

50
00:06:39,337 --> 00:06:55,200
He calculated perinatal mortality rates using only births in a given week of gestation from 37 to 43 completed weeks compared with the cumulative probability—the perinatal index—of death when all ongoing pregnancies are included in the denominator.

51
00:06:55,200 --> 00:07:02,175
Using this computation, delivery at 38 weeks’ gestation had the lowest risk for perinatal death.

52
00:07:02,175 --> 00:07:08,500
In current practice, perinatal death is balanced against morbidity associated with immaturity.

53
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:16,562
This shapes the rationale for delaying delivery until 39 weeks’ gestation unless a comorbidity warrants earlier intervention .

54
00:07:16,562 --> 00:07:18,800
Pathophysiology.

55
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:21,662
# Postmaturity Syndrome.

56
00:07:21,662 --> 00:07:36,137
The postmature newborn is unique, and features include wrinkled, patchy, peeling skin; a long, thin body that suggests wasting; and advanced maturity in that the neonate is open-eyed, unusually alert, and appears old and worried .

57
00:07:36,137 --> 00:07:41,762
Skin wrinkling can be prominent on the palms and soles, and nails are typically long.

58
00:07:41,762 --> 00:07:50,562
Most postmature neonates are not technically growth restricted because their birthweight seldom falls below the 10th percentile for gestational age .

59
00:07:50,562 --> 00:07:58,162
However, severe growth restriction— which logically must have preceded completion of 42 weeks—may be present.

60
00:07:58,162 --> 00:08:00,700
Figure 46-2.

61
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:03,012
Postmaturity syndrome.

62
00:08:03,012 --> 00:08:10,062
Neonate delivered at 43 weeks’ gestation with thick, viscous meconium coating the desquamating skin.

63
00:08:10,062 --> 00:08:14,025
Note the long, thin appearance and wrinkling of the hands.

64
00:08:14,025 --> 00:08:29,062
The incidence of postmaturity syndrome in newborns at 41, 42, or 43 weeks, respectively, has not been conclusively determined, but the syndrome complicates 10 to 20 percent of pregnancies at 42 completed weeks .

65
00:08:29,062 --> 00:08:34,837
Associated oligohydramnios substantially raises the likelihood of postmaturity.

66
00:08:34,837 --> 00:08:51,050
Trimmer and associates (1990) reported that 88 percent of fetuses were postmature if there was oligohydramnios, defined by a sonographic maximal vertical amnionic fluid pocket that measured ≤1 cm at 42 weeks.

67
00:08:51,050 --> 00:08:53,800
# Placental Dysfunction.

68
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:58,137
Many believe that postterm pregnancy is an abnormal state.

69
00:08:58,137 --> 00:09:10,675
Redman and Staff (2015) suggest that limited placental capacity, characterized by dysfunctional syncytiotrophoblast, explains the greater risks of the postmaturity syndrome.

70
00:09:10,675 --> 00:09:14,900
A murine model of stillborn fetuses supports this theory .

71
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:23,712
Clifford (1954) proposed that the associated skin changes follow loss of the protective effects of vernix caseosa.

72
00:09:23,712 --> 00:09:32,250
He also attributed the postmaturity syndrome to placental senescence, although he did not find placental degeneration histologically.

73
00:09:32,250 --> 00:09:49,100
Still, the concept that postmaturity stems from placental insufficiency has persisted despite an absence of morphological or significant quantitative findings (Larsen, 1995; Redman, 2015; Rushton, 1991).

74
00:09:49,100 --> 00:10:00,375
However, the rate of placental apoptosis—programmed cell death— is significantly greater at 41 to 42 completed weeks compared with that at 36 to 39

75
00:10:00,532 --> 00:10:02,057
weeks .

76
00:10:02,007 --> 00:10:10,869
Several proapoptotic genes such as kisspeptin are upregulated in postterm placental explants compared with the same genes in term ones .

77
00:10:10,869 --> 00:10:15,057
The clinical significance of such apoptosis is unclear.

78
00:10:15,057 --> 00:10:27,357
Jazayeri and coworkers (1998) investigated cord blood erythropoietin levels in 124 appropriately grown newborns delivered from 37 to 43 weeks.

79
00:10:27,357 --> 00:10:33,007
The only known stimulator of erythropoietin is decreased partial oxygen pressure.

80
00:10:33,007 --> 00:10:40,819
Thus, they sought to assess whether fetal oxygenation was compromised due to placental aging in postterm pregnancies.

81
00:10:40,819 --> 00:10:44,669
All women had an uncomplicated labor and delivery.

82
00:10:44,669 --> 00:10:51,832
Cord blood erythropoietin levels were significantly elevated in pregnancies reaching 41 weeks or more.

83
00:10:51,832 --> 00:11:01,294
Although Apgar scores and acid-base studies were normal, these researchers concluded that fetal oxygenation was decreased in some postterm gestations.

84
00:11:01,294 --> 00:11:07,982
However, the postterm fetus typically continues to gain weight and thus be unusually large at birth.

85
00:11:07,982 --> 00:11:12,732
This suggests that placental function is not severely compromised.

86
00:11:12,732 --> 00:11:19,982
Indeed, continued fetal growth is the norm—albeit at a slower rate beginning at 37 completed weeks .

87
00:11:19,982 --> 00:11:27,519
Nahum and associates (1995) confirmed that fetal growth continues until at least 42 weeks.

88
00:11:27,519 --> 00:11:32,132
However, umbilical blood flow does not increase concomitantly .

89
00:11:32,132 --> 00:11:34,682
Figure 46-3.

90
00:11:34,682 --> 00:11:38,844
Mean daily fetal growth during previous week of gestation.

91
00:11:38,844 --> 00:11:45,494
Redrawn from Hendricks CH: Patterns of fetal and placental growth: the second half of pregnancy.

92
00:11:45,494 --> 00:11:50,807
Obstet Gynecol 24:357, 1964.

93
00:11:50,807 --> 00:11:54,619
# Fetal Distress and Oligohydramnios.

94
00:11:54,619 --> 00:12:02,482
The principal reasons for increased risks to postterm fetuses were described by Leveno and colleagues (1984).

95
00:12:02,482 --> 00:12:12,519
Both antepartum fetal jeopardy and intrapartum nonreassuring fetal status were found to be the consequence of cord compression associated with oligohydramnios.

96
00:12:12,519 --> 00:12:27,107
In their analysis of 727 postterm pregnancies, intrapartum nonreassuring fetal status detected with electronic monitoring was not associated with late decelerations characteristic of uteroplacental insufficiency.

97
00:12:27,107 --> 00:12:38,957
Instead, one or more prolonged decelerations such as shown in Figure 46-4 preceded three fourths of emergency cesarean deliveries for nonreassuring fetal heart rate tracings.

98
00:12:38,957 --> 00:12:43,669
In all but two cases, there were also variable decelerations.

99
00:12:43,669 --> 00:12:50,444
Another common fetal heart rate pattern, although not ominous by itself, was the saltatory baseline.

100
00:12:50,444 --> 00:13:00,682
As described in Chapter 24 (p. 453), these findings are consistent with cord occlusion as the proximate cause of the nonreassuring tracings.

101
00:13:00,682 --> 00:13:05,594
Other correlates included oligohydramnios and viscous meconium.

102
00:13:05,594 --> 00:13:17,744
Schaffer and coworkers (2005) implicated a nuchal cord in abnormal intrapartum fetal heart rate patterns, meconium, and compromised newborn condition in prolonged pregnancies.

103
00:13:17,744 --> 00:13:20,419
Figure 46-4.

104
00:13:20,419 --> 00:13:28,869
A. Prolonged fetal heart rate deceleration before emergency cesarean delivery in a postterm pregnancy with oligohydramnios.

105
00:13:28,869 --> 00:13:34,494
B. Variable decelerations in a postterm pregnancy with oligohydramnios.

106
00:13:34,494 --> 00:13:44,357
C. Saltatory baseline fetal heart rate showing oscillations exceeding 20 bpm and associated with oligohydramnios in a postterm pregnancy.

107
00:13:44,357 --> 00:14:04,607
Reproduced with permission from Leveno KJ, Quirk JG, Cunningham FG, et al: Prolonged pregnancy, I. Observations concerning the causes of fetal distress, Am J Obstet Gynecol. 1984 Nov 1;150(5 Pt 1):465–473.

108
00:14:04,607 --> 00:14:13,982
Normally, the volume of amnionic fluid continues to decline after 38 weeks, and logically oligohydramnios may become problematic.

109
00:14:13,982 --> 00:14:24,144
Moreover, meconium release into an already reduced amnionic fluid volume results in thick, viscous fluid that may cause meconium aspiration syndrome .

110
00:14:24,144 --> 00:14:28,494
The risk for this increases to 5 percent at 42 weeks .

111
00:14:28,494 --> 00:14:31,332
# Fetal-growth Restriction.

112
00:14:31,332 --> 00:14:40,744
In the late 1990s, the clinical significance of fetal-growth restriction in the otherwise uncomplicated pregnancy became more fully appreciated.

113
00:14:40,744 --> 00:14:54,494
Data from the National Swedish Medical Birth Registry showed that stillbirths were more common among growth-restricted newborns who were delivered after 42 weeks (Clausson, 1999; Divon, 1998).

114
00:14:54,494 --> 00:14:59,407
Indeed, a third of postterm stillborn neonates were growth restricted.

115
00:14:59,407 --> 00:15:03,132
During this time in Sweden, labor induction and

116
00:15:03,292 --> 00:15:07,779
antenatal fetal testing usually commenced at 42 weeks.

117
00:15:07,729 --> 00:15:21,929
In a study from Parkland Hospital, Alexander and colleagues (2000d) analyzed outcomes for 355 neonates from gestations ≥42 weeks and with birthweights <3rd percentile.

118
00:15:21,929 --> 00:15:29,704
These outcomes were compared with those of 14,520 similarly aged newborns above the 3rd percentile.

119
00:15:29,704 --> 00:15:35,379
Morbidity and mortality rates were significantly higher in the growth-restricted neonates.

120
00:15:35,379 --> 00:15:44,229
Moreover, a fourth of all stillbirths associated with prolonged pregnancy were in this comparatively small number of growth-restricted fetuses.

121
00:15:44,229 --> 00:15:46,179
Complications.

122
00:15:46,179 --> 00:15:48,967
# Oligohydramnios.

123
00:15:48,967 --> 00:15:55,279
Recognized postterm complications include oligohydramnios and fetal macrosomia.

124
00:15:55,279 --> 00:16:06,279
In 38 postterm pregnancies, Trimmer and colleagues (1990) sonographically measured hourly fetal urine production using sequential bladder volume measurements.

125
00:16:06,279 --> 00:16:11,417
Diminished urine production was found to be associated with oligohydramnios.

126
00:16:11,417 --> 00:16:22,717
Using Doppler waveforms, Oz and associates (2002) concluded that fetal renal blood flow is reduced in postterm pregnancies complicated by oligohydramnios.

127
00:16:22,717 --> 00:16:27,854
The lack of increasing umbilical blood flow postterm may be a possible cause .

128
00:16:27,854 --> 00:16:34,179
Diminished amnionic fluid volume at any gestational age signifies increased fetal risk.

129
00:16:34,179 --> 00:16:39,579
Unfortunately, an exact method to define this decreased volume is lacking.

130
00:16:39,579 --> 00:16:48,679
Currently, sonographic measurement of the deepest vertical pocket or calculation of the amnionic fluid index (AFI) are options .

131
00:16:48,679 --> 00:16:57,754
One study of postterm pregnancies attempted to determine which amnionic fluid volume criteria best predicted normal and abnormal outcomes .

132
00:16:57,754 --> 00:17:05,029
The smaller the amnionic fluid pocket, the greater the likelihood of clinically significant oligohydramnios.

133
00:17:05,029 --> 00:17:10,679
However, normal amnionic fluid volume did not preclude abnormal outcomes.

134
00:17:10,679 --> 00:17:19,479
One randomized trial assigned 500 women with postterm pregnancies to assessment using either AFI or the deepest vertical pocket .

135
00:17:19,479 --> 00:17:26,667
The authors concluded that the AFI overestimated the number of abnormal outcomes in postterm pregnancies.

136
00:17:26,667 --> 00:17:38,367
Regardless of the criteria used to diagnose oligohydramnios in postterm pregnancies, most investigators have found a higher incidence of some measure of “fetal compromise” during labor.

137
00:17:38,367 --> 00:17:43,967
Thus, oligohydramnios by most definitions is a clinically meaningful finding.

138
00:17:43,967 --> 00:17:51,479
Conversely, reassurance of continued fetal well-being in the presence of “normal” amnionic fluid volume is tenuous.

139
00:17:51,479 --> 00:17:57,104
This may be related to how quickly pathological oligohydramnios can develop.

140
00:17:57,104 --> 00:17:59,479
# Macrosomia.

141
00:17:59,479 --> 00:18:06,492
The velocity of fetal weight gain peaks at approximately 37 weeks (see Fig. 46-3).

142
00:18:06,492 --> 00:18:12,054
Although growth velocity slows at that time, most fetuses continue to gain weight.

143
00:18:12,054 --> 00:18:21,342
According to Duryea and associates (2014), the 95th percentile at 42 weeks is 4475 g.

144
00:18:21,342 --> 00:18:27,767
Even so, in one study, brachial plexus injury was not related to postterm gestation .

145
00:18:27,767 --> 00:18:37,404
Intuitively, it seems that both maternal and fetal morbidity associated with macrosomia would be mitigated with timely induction to preempt further growth.

146
00:18:37,404 --> 00:18:41,104
This, however, does not appear to be the case.

147
00:18:41,104 --> 00:18:52,817
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) concludes that current evidence does not support such a practice in women at term with suspected fetal macrosomia.

148
00:18:52,817 --> 00:19:03,304
Moreover, the College notes that in the absence of diabetes, vaginal delivery is not contraindicated for women with an estimated fetal weight up to 5000 g .

149
00:19:03,304 --> 00:19:09,842
Obvious problems with all such recommendations are substantive variations in fetal weight estimation.

150
00:19:09,842 --> 00:19:12,167
Antepartum Management.

151
00:19:12,167 --> 00:19:20,042
After completing 42 weeks, labor induction is recommended to help avoid the just-described morbidity and mortality.

152
00:19:20,042 --> 00:19:26,779
For late-term gestations, some intervention is indicated, but the method and timing are not unanimous.

153
00:19:26,779 --> 00:19:34,004
The decision focuses on whether labor induction is warranted or if expectant management with fetal surveillance is best.

154
00:19:34,004 --> 00:19:45,754
In a survey done more than 15 years ago, 73 percent of members of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists reported routinely induced women at 41 weeks .

155
00:19:45,754 --> 00:19:52,479
Most of the remainder performed twice-weekly fetal antepartum testing until 42 completed weeks.

156
00:19:52,479 --> 00:19:54,179
#

157
00:19:54,340 --> 00:19:56,515
Induction Factors.

158
00:19:56,465 --> 00:20:02,177
A cervix that is soft, dilated, and effaced improves labor induction success.

159
00:20:02,177 --> 00:20:08,302
However, investigators have used differing criteria in studies of prolonged pregnancy.

160
00:20:08,302 --> 00:20:18,852
Harris and coworkers (1983) defined an unfavorable cervix by a Bishop score <7 and reported this in 92 percent of women at 42 weeks .

161
00:20:18,852 --> 00:20:25,227
Others found that 40 percent of women with a 41-week gestation had an “undilated cervix” .

162
00:20:25,227 --> 00:20:36,865
In a study of 800 women undergoing induction for postterm pregnancy at Parkland Hospital, women without cervical dilation had a twofold higher cesarean delivery rate for “dystocia” .

163
00:20:36,865 --> 00:20:45,490
Instead, a cervical length ≤3 cm measured with transvaginal sonography predicted successful induction .

164
00:20:45,490 --> 00:20:54,940
In a similar study, cervical length ≤25 mm positively predicted spontaneous labor or successful induction .

165
00:20:54,940 --> 00:21:07,752
Several investigators have evaluated prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) and E1 (PGE1) for induction in women with an unfavorable cervix and postterm pregnancy.

166
00:21:07,752 --> 00:21:17,015
A study by the Maternal–Fetal Medicine Units Network (1994) found that PGE2 gel was not more effective than placebo.

167
00:21:17,015 --> 00:21:35,740
Alexander and associates (2000c) treated 393 women with a postterm pregnancy with PGE2, regardless of cervical “favorability,” and reported that almost half of the 84 women with cervical dilation of 2 to 4 cm entered labor with PGE2 use alone.

168
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:43,402
Prostaglandins and other agents used for cervical ripening are discussed in Chapter 26 (p. 488).

169
00:21:43,402 --> 00:21:50,527
Sweeping or stripping of the membranes to induce labor and thereby prevent postterm pregnancy has been evaluated.

170
00:21:50,527 --> 00:21:58,615
A metaanalysis of 44 studies found that membrane stripping slightly increased spontaneous labor and lowered induction rates .

171
00:21:58,615 --> 00:22:03,315
However, this practice did not lower the cesarean delivery rate.

172
00:22:03,315 --> 00:22:13,815
Other trials have found that sweeping membranes did not reduce the need to induce labor (Hill, 2008; Kashanian 2006; Wong, 2002).

173
00:22:13,815 --> 00:22:20,952
Drawbacks of membrane stripping included pain, vaginal bleeding, and irregular contractions without labor.

174
00:22:20,952 --> 00:22:27,690
The station of the fetal head within the pelvis is another predictor of successful postterm pregnancy induction.

175
00:22:27,690 --> 00:22:36,290
Shin and colleagues (2004) studied 484 nulliparas who underwent induction after 41 weeks’ gestation.

176
00:22:36,290 --> 00:22:40,540
The cesarean delivery rate was directly related to station.

177
00:22:40,540 --> 00:22:53,615
The rate was 6 percent if the vertex before induction was at –1 station; 20 percent at –2 station; 43 percent at –3 station; and 77 percent at –4 station.

178
00:22:53,615 --> 00:22:56,890
# Induction versus Fetal Testing.

179
00:22:56,890 --> 00:23:07,215
The less than optimal benefits from induction with an unfavorable cervix lead some clinicians to prefer fetal antepartum testing beginning at 41 completed weeks.

180
00:23:07,215 --> 00:23:16,940
In a Canadian study, 3407 women were randomly assigned at ≥41 weeks to induction or to fetal testing .

181
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:27,665
Labor induction resulted in a small but significant reduction in the cesarean delivery rate compared with fetal testing—21 versus 24 percent, respectively.

182
00:23:27,665 --> 00:23:31,565
There were only two stillbirths in the fetal testing group.

183
00:23:31,565 --> 00:23:40,490
The Maternal–Fetal Medicine Network then performed a randomized trial of induction versus fetal testing beginning at 41 weeks’ gestation .

184
00:23:40,490 --> 00:23:50,490
Fetal surveillance included nonstress testing and sonographic estimation of amnionic fluid volume performed twice weekly in 175 women.

185
00:23:50,490 --> 00:24:00,327
Perinatal outcomes were compared with those of 265 women also at 41 weeks but randomly assigned to induction with or without cervical ripening.

186
00:24:00,327 --> 00:24:06,315
There were no perinatal deaths, and the cesarean delivery rate did not differ between groups.

187
00:24:06,315 --> 00:24:10,990
The results of this study support the validity of either management scheme.

188
00:24:10,990 --> 00:24:27,165
In another study of women reaching 41 completed weeks’ gestation, those who entered spontaneous labor prior to 420/7 weeks had a lower cesarean delivery rate than those who did not spontaneously labor but who underwent induction after 420/7 weeks .

189
00:24:27,165 --> 00:24:35,977
Cesarean delivery rates were significantly increased—19 versus 14 percent—in the induced group because of failure to progress.

190
00:24:35,977 --> 00:24:39,265
A study from Denmark is also instructive .

191
00:24:39,265 --> 00:24:50,727
In 2011, the Danish national guidelines were changed from labor induction at 420/7 weeks with no fetal surveillance to labor induction at 412/7

192
00:24:50,884 --> 00:24:57,034
to 416/7 weeks with fetal surveillance beginning at 410/7 weeks.

193
00:24:56,984 --> 00:25:02,496
They compared two 3-year epochs— one before and one after 2011.

194
00:25:02,496 --> 00:25:10,659
The rate of pregnancies that progressed past 420/7 weeks decreased from 2.85 to 0.62 percent.

195
00:25:10,659 --> 00:25:21,984
Concurrently, as expected, the induction rate rose significantly, and this was accompanied by a drop in the perinatal mortality rate—22 to 13 per 1000 births.

196
00:25:21,984 --> 00:25:25,409
The cesarean delivery rate was not changed.

197
00:25:25,409 --> 00:25:39,496
A similar before-and-after observational study reported that induction at ≥42 weeks’ gestation was associated with a significantly lower cesarean delivery rate—15 versus 19.4 percent .

198
00:25:39,496 --> 00:25:52,159
To further address the question, the SWEdish Post-term Induction Study —SWEPIS—was a randomized trial that included 2760 low-risk pregnancies at 41 weeks’ gestation.

199
00:25:52,159 --> 00:26:00,384
The trial compared outcomes with induction of labor at 41 weeks against expectant management and induction at 42 weeks.

200
00:26:00,384 --> 00:26:09,496
As shown in Table 46-2, maternal and perinatal outcomes were not different with the exception of perinatal mortality and macrosomia .

201
00:26:09,496 --> 00:26:16,859
The study was stopped early because of a significantly higher perinatal mortality rate in the expectantly management group.

202
00:26:16,859 --> 00:26:25,546
The investigators concluded that these results should be interpreted with caution because the primary composite outcomes were not significantly different.

203
00:26:25,546 --> 00:26:28,109
Table 46-2.

204
00:26:28,109 --> 00:26:34,284
Selected Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes from SWEPIS Trial # Management Strategies.

205
00:26:34,284 --> 00:26:41,684
At this time, evidence is insufficient to mandate a management strategy between 40 and 42 completed weeks.

206
00:26:41,684 --> 00:26:49,321
Thus, induction of labor or initiation of fetal surveillance at 41 weeks’ gestation is a reasonable option.

207
00:26:49,321 --> 00:27:03,621
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2021) suggest that fetal surveillance should be done once or twice weekly beginning at 41 0/7 weeks.

208
00:27:03,621 --> 00:27:08,296
After completing 42 weeks, labor induction is recommended.

209
00:27:08,296 --> 00:27:18,084
A management algorithm by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) is summarized in Figure 46-5.

210
00:27:18,084 --> 00:27:30,509
In light of the recent studies that show an increased stillbirth rate associated with expectant management at 41 weeks, it is likely that routine induction will eventually become preferable in most circumstances.

211
00:27:30,509 --> 00:27:33,196
Figure 46-5.

212
00:27:33,196 --> 00:27:36,934
Algorithm for management of postterm pregnancy.

213
00:27:36,934 --> 00:27:50,709
When gestational age is uncertain, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) recommends delivery at 41 weeks’ gestation using the best clinical estimate of gestational age.

214
00:27:50,709 --> 00:27:55,971
The College also recommends against amniocentesis for fetal lung maturity.

215
00:27:55,971 --> 00:27:58,234
Intrapartum Management.

216
00:27:58,234 --> 00:28:02,759
Labor is a particularly dangerous time for the postterm fetus.

217
00:28:02,759 --> 00:28:10,634
Thus, women whose pregnancies are known or suspected to be postterm ideally come to the hospital as soon as they suspect labor.

218
00:28:10,634 --> 00:28:20,646
While being evaluated for active labor, fetal heart rate and uterine contractions are monitored electronically for variations consistent with fetal compromise .

219
00:28:20,646 --> 00:28:25,521
During labor, the decision to perform amniotomy is problematic.

220
00:28:25,521 --> 00:28:31,984
Further reduction in fluid volume following amniotomy can enhance the possibility of cord compression.

221
00:28:31,984 --> 00:28:38,946
Conversely, after membrane rupture, a scalp electrode and an intrauterine pressure catheter can be placed.

222
00:28:38,946 --> 00:28:45,071
These usually provide more precise data concerning fetal heart rate and uterine contractions.

223
00:28:45,071 --> 00:28:49,309
Amniotomy also aids identification of thick meconium.

224
00:28:49,309 --> 00:28:53,796
Thick meconium in the amnionic fluid is particularly worrisome.

225
00:28:53,796 --> 00:28:59,371
The viscosity probably signifies the lack of liquid and thus oligohydramnios.

226
00:28:59,371 --> 00:29:05,359
Aspiration of thick meconium may cause severe pulmonary dysfunction and neonatal death .

227
00:29:05,359 --> 00:29:15,359
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a), amnioinfusion does not prevent meconium aspiration syndrome.

228
00:29:15,359 --> 00:29:24,171
However, it remains a reasonable treatment approach for repetitive variable decelerations (Chap, 24, p. 459).

229
00:29:24,171 --> 00:29:34,959
Suctioning the pharynx once the head is delivered is not recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Wyckoff, 2020).

230
00:29:34,959 --> 00:29:40,309
If the depressed newborn has meconium-stained fluid, intubation is performed.

231
00:29:40,309 --> 00:29:42,421
The likelihood of a

232
00:29:42,580 --> 00:29:51,467
successful vaginal delivery is reduced appreciably for the nullipara who is in early labor with thick, meconium-stained amnionic fluid.

233
00:29:51,417 --> 00:30:05,667
Thus, if a woman is remote from delivery, strong consideration should be given to prompt cesarean delivery, especially when cephalopelvic disproportion is suspected or either hypotonic or hypertonic dysfunctional labor is evident.

234
00:30:05,667 --> 00:30:10,392
Some practitioners choose to avoid oxytocin use in these cases.

